"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunkp_vs_np_docs=globalThis.webpackChunkp_vs_np_docs||[]).push([[2807],{26948(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"tag":{"label":"optimization","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/tags/optimization","allTagsPath":"/azeon/p-vs-np/tags","count":6,"items":[{"id":"real-world-impact/machine-learning-constraints","title":"Machine Learning Constraints","description":"Training even a tiny neural network perfectly is NP-Hard. Yet machine learning works astonishingly well. The reason is the same lesson again: training never asks for the perfect answer, and real data isn\'t the worst case.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/real-world-impact/machine-learning-constraints"},{"id":"complexity-zoo/np-hard-vs-np-complete","title":"NP-Hard vs. NP-Complete","description":"Two labels I\'d been using almost interchangeably. NP-Complete means \'hardest inside NP\'. NP-Hard only means \'at least that hard\', and some NP-Hard problems can\'t be solved by any computer, ever.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/complexity-zoo/np-hard-vs-np-complete"},{"id":"real-world-impact/p-vs-np-in-the-boardroom","title":"P vs NP in the Boardroom","description":"Why should someone running a company care about a math problem? Because the moment a product promises the \'best\' schedule, route or match, it has probably walked into NP-Hard territory, and the way you handle that is a business decision.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/real-world-impact/p-vs-np-in-the-boardroom"},{"id":"real-world-impact/the-cost-of-inefficiency","title":"The Cost of Inefficiency","description":"How much money does the world lose because we can\'t solve NP-Hard problems perfectly? I went looking for the number. Nobody can honestly calculate it, and the reason is itself a lesson about NP-Hardness.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/real-world-impact/the-cost-of-inefficiency"},{"id":"np-completeness/the-knapsack-problem","title":"The Knapsack Problem","description":"A bag with a weight limit and a pile of things worth different amounts. The obvious strategy fails, the clever strategy is secretly exponential, and an entire encryption scheme was once built on top of it and broken.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/np-completeness/the-knapsack-problem"},{"id":"np-completeness/the-traveling-salesman-problem","title":"The Traveling Salesman Problem","description":"Write a number on every road from the last post and ask for the shortest round trip. That one change gives you the most famous hard problem in computer science, and a reduction you can do in your head.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/np-completeness/the-traveling-salesman-problem"}],"unlisted":false}}')}}]);